Relevant columns and why they matter  
(posts_questions)  
• `id` : primary key of the question – used to join with answers.  
• `creation_date` : timestamp when the question was asked – used to (a) restrict to 2021 and (b) derive day‑of‑week.  
• `answer_count` : optional quick check whether a question ever got an answer.  

(posts_answers)  
• `parent_id` : id of the question that an answer belongs to – the join key.  
• `creation_date` : timestamp when the answer was posted – used to find the first answer and to measure the time gap to the question.  

Typical conditions  
• `WHERE q.creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'` – only 2021 questions.  
• `AND a.creation_date <= TIMESTAMP_ADD(q.creation_date, INTERVAL 1 HOUR)` – answers coming within one hour.  

Below are ten exploratory → incremental queries (each limited to 20 rows except the last, which still fits in the 20‑row rule because it returns only the 7 days of the week).

```sql
-- 1. List a few 2021 questions with their creation timestamps
SELECT id, creation_date, answer_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions` AS q
WHERE creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show the weekday number (1=Sun … 7=Sat) for those questions
SELECT id,
       creation_date,
       EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM creation_date) AS weekday_num
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions` AS q
WHERE creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Distinct weekday numbers actually present in 2021 questions
SELECT DISTINCT EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM creation_date) AS weekday_num
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
ORDER BY weekday_num
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Peek at a few answers, together with their parent question id
SELECT parent_id, id AS answer_id, creation_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join questions to their first answer (min answer time) in 2021
SELECT q.id,
       q.creation_date AS question_time,
       MIN(a.creation_date) AS first_answer_time
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions` AS q
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`  AS a
  ON a.parent_id = q.id
WHERE q.creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
GROUP BY q.id, q.creation_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Add the gap in minutes between question and first answer
SELECT q.id,
       q.creation_date,
       TIMESTAMP_DIFF(MIN(a.creation_date), q.creation_date, MINUTE) AS minutes_to_first_answer
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions` AS q
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`  AS a
  ON a.parent_id = q.id
WHERE q.creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
GROUP BY q.id, q.creation_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Flag whether a question was answered within 60 minutes
SELECT q.id,
       TIMESTAMP_DIFF(MIN(a.creation_date), q.creation_date, MINUTE) AS minutes_to_first_answer,
       TIMESTAMP_DIFF(MIN(a.creation_date), q.creation_date, MINUTE) <= 60 AS answered_within_hour
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions` AS q
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`  AS a
  ON a.parent_id = q.id
WHERE q.creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
GROUP BY q.id, q.creation_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Count total 2021 questions per weekday (no answer info yet)
SELECT EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM creation_date) AS weekday_num,
       COUNT(*) AS questions_2021
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
GROUP BY weekday_num
ORDER BY weekday_num
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Count how many 2021 questions received an answer within 1 hour, per weekday
WITH first_answer AS (
  SELECT q.id,
         q.creation_date,
         EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM q.creation_date) AS weekday_num,
         MIN(a.creation_date) AS first_answer_time
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions` AS q
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers`  AS a
    ON a.parent_id = q.id
  WHERE q.creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
  GROUP BY q.id, q.creation_date
)
SELECT weekday_num,
       COUNTIF(TIMESTAMP_DIFF(first_answer_time, creation_date, MINUTE) <= 60) AS answered_within_hour
FROM first_answer
GROUP BY weekday_num
ORDER BY weekday_num
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL RESULT: questions per weekday in 2021, how many & % answered within 1 hour
WITH q21 AS (
  SELECT id,
         creation_date,
         EXTRACT(DAYOFWEEK FROM creation_date)               AS weekday_num
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
  WHERE creation_date BETWEEN '2021-01-01' AND '2022-01-01'
),
first_answer AS (
  SELECT q.id,
         q.weekday_num,
         MIN(a.creation_date) AS first_answer_time
  FROM q21 AS q
  LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_answers` AS a
    ON a.parent_id = q.id
  GROUP BY q.id, q.weekday_num
)
SELECT weekday_num,
       COUNT(*)                                           AS total_questions,
       COUNTIF(TIMESTAMP_DIFF(first_answer_time,
                              (SELECT creation_date FROM q21 WHERE id = fa.id LIMIT 1),
                              MINUTE) <= 60)              AS answered_within_hour,
       ROUND(100 * COUNTIF(TIMESTAMP_DIFF(first_answer_time,
                              (SELECT creation_date FROM q21 WHERE id = fa.id LIMIT 1),
                              MINUTE) <= 60)
             / COUNT(*), 2)                               AS pct_within_hour
FROM first_answer AS fa
GROUP BY weekday_num
ORDER BY weekday_num
LIMIT 20;
--Description:
--Returns, for each day of week (1=Sun … 7=Sat), the number of Stack Overflow
--questions asked in 2021, how many got a first answer within one hour, and
--the corresponding percentage.
```

